{"artifact_id":"Figure2","kind":"figure","asset_path":"figures/fig_2506.07853v1-Figure2-1-d013f398f34f.png","asset_sha256":"d013f398f34f74b2d7866a572206fc09f738b33f52a4b20e5940df0e12f10932","source_pdf_sha256":"bd601ac01957bfcb5ccaae523ecf11d324d21e2a6bb59ea8a094f1e6a1a8e906","revision_vid":"ecd9b6cd9f101cbe205827de598835d2aeb100f6184931ae36a8e2304f97b529","canonical_id":"2506.07853","section_vid":"8f6dff408c2c1ddfa7844019b1f08dbc89ee6a926559e089242e201bded36d3f","detector_fingerprint":"pdffigures2-0.0.12-SNAPSHOT","detector_metadata_sha256":"7a366ebfefd5e0db418c76730c263107d43c6df43be2a8755518a6e3e5020532","page_index":7,"region":{"x1":94.56,"y1":70.56,"x2":517.4399999999999,"y2":322.08},"caption":"Figure 2: Monolingual case (Portuguese): Temporal Versions (TV) and Language Versions (LV) coincide.","containing_section":{"relation":"containing","section_id":"sec-1~5","path_id":"1~5","title":"Figure 2 :","text":"2Figure 2: Monolingual case (Portuguese): Temporal Versions (TV) and Language Versions (LV) coincide.","truncated":false,"section_ordinal":18},"neighboring_sections":[{"relation":"previous","section_id":"sec-1~4","path_id":"1~4","title":"Figure 1 :","text":"1Figure 1: Relationship among Norm (Work), TemporalVersion (F22 SelfContainedExpression), LanguageVersion (F22 SelfContainedExpression), and Manifestation.","truncated":false,"section_ordinal":17},{"relation":"next","section_id":"sec-1~6","path_id":"1~6","title":"Figure 3 :","text":"3Figure 3: Relationships among Temporal Versions reflecting the compilation process.","truncated":false,"section_ordinal":19}],"reference_excerpts":[{"section_id":"sec-3.1","path_id":"3.1","title":"Practical Mapping of Work, Expression, and Manifestation","matched_form":"figure 2","excerpt":"uage Version as explicit subclasses of &lt;F22 SelfContained Expression&gt;, we maintain strict alignment with FRBRoo while clarifying that each \"version\" is, in fact, a specialized kind of Expression.In jurisdictions with multiple official languages-such as Canada or the European Union-a legal norm's Temporal Version is inherently multilingual, comprising one distinct Language Version for each official language.In contrast, in monolingual jurisdictions (including Brazil), each Temporal Version is effectively monolingual, so the single Language Version coincides with the Temporal Version (see Figure 2).The Signed Document of the original version-whether a physical paper with a handwritten signature or a digitally signed authoritative file-depicted in Figure 2 is the first and most authoritative Manifestation of that version. In FRBRoo terms, this is an &lt;F4 ManifestationSingleton&gt; if it is a unique physical object.The Official Publication of the original version (e.g., in the Official Gazette) is a subsequent Manifestation, intended for public dissemination and to confer broader legal effect or notice. It reproduces the content of the original Expression, typically as an &lt;F","truncated":true},{"section_id":"sec-1~5","path_id":"1~5","title":"Figure 2 :","matched_form":"figure 2","excerpt":"2Figure 2: Monolingual case (Portuguese): Temporal Versions (TV) and Language Versions (LV) coincide.","truncated":false}],"article_summary":{"source_id":"grobid:2506.07853#abstract","source_text_sha256":"cdaeefbc29177ef1f5519a4453f30b9929a891d2bdd5d6f6fa5becedba41a7ab","text":"Effectively representing legal norms for automated processing is a critical challenge, particularly in tracking the diachronic evolution of their hierarchical components (e.g., articles, paragraphs). While foundational frameworks like FRBR/FRBRoo and standards like Akoma Ntoso model legal documents at a macro level, they lack native mechanisms for granular, component-level versioning. This limitation hinders the deterministic point-in-time reconstruction of legal texts, a fundamental capability for reliable Legal Tech and AI applications. This paper proposes a structured, temporal model that extends the FRBRoo framework to address this gap. It introduces specialized subclasses of Expression-Temporal Version (TV) and Language Version (LV)-to represent the state of a legal norm and its linguistic variations at specific points in time. The model applies this same paradigm hierarchically, introducing Component Work (CW), Component Temporal Version (CTV), and Component Language Version (CLV) to track the lifecycle of individual articles, paragraphs, and clauses. Using the Brazilian Federal Constitution as a case study, the paper demonstrates how each amendment creates new Component Temporal Versions for affected provisions, while unaffected components retain their existing versions. This fine-grained, time-aware architecture enables the precise, deterministic retrieval and reconstruction of any part of a legal text as it existed on a specific date. The model provides a robust foundation for developing advanced legal information systems, knowledge graphs, and AI tools capable of accurate historical analysis and impact assessment, overcoming the limitations of current generative models.","truncated":false},"diagnostics":[],"total_context_chars":3449,"truncated":true}